"""Report part B: verification and validation, TEA, LCA, scenarios, sensitivity, break-even, prospective."""
from ctx import *
from doclib import f0, f1, f2, pct
import registry as REG

def vv(d):
    d.h1("5. Model verification and validation", bm="sec_vv", pb=True)
    d.p("Verification asks whether a model computes what it is meant to compute; validation asks whether what it computes agrees with independent evidence. Both were applied twice: first to the inherited 2018 balance and financial model, then to the new engine.")
    d.h2("5.1 Verification of the inherited balance")
    d.p(f"{NVER} quantities and properties of the original design were recomputed from stoichiometry, published productivity, thermodynamics and the workbooks themselves. Each check is traced to the workbook sheet and cell or the thesis page it rests on (Source column of «tab:verify»); the inherited values and their locations are listed in the database (sheet 03_Original_Data, {len(INH)} entries). The original 2018 and 2021 records were checked as received; they have not been re-verified against the authors' archived originals. «fig:F03_verification» summarises the outcome and «tab:verify» gives every check.")
    d.fig("F03_verification")
    rows = [[c["no"], c["quantity"], c["inherited"], c["recomputed"], c["verdict"], c["severity"], c["source"]] for c in VER]
    d.table("verify", "Verification of the inherited 2018 balance and financial model, with the location of each inherited value.", ["No.", "Quantity", "Inherited", "Recomputed", "Verdict", "Severity", "Source (sheet, cell or page)"], rows, widths=[0.35, 1.15, 1.5, 1.75, 0.5, 0.55, 1.6], size=12)
    npass = sum(1 for c in VER if c["verdict"] == "Pass"); nhigh = sum(1 for c in VER if c["verdict"] == "Fail" and c["severity"] == "High")
    d.p(f"{npass} checks pass and {len(VER)-npass} do not hold, {nhigh} of them with high severity. Four findings change the answer. First, the algae are fed, not grown: stream 1 of the balance brings 79.5 t/d of biomass into the plant as starting culture, and the ponds add 0.5 t/d. Producing 80 t/d in 10.36 ha would require 772 g/m²/d, and in the 3.98 ha of the plant layout 2,008 g/m²/d, against 10–21 g/m²/d measured outdoors («fig:F02_productivity_check») [@klein2024,huntley2015,jo2020]. Second, the HTL reactor has no heat duty, although bringing the slurry to 350 °C needs 7.5 MW gross. Third, the tri- and diglycerides leaving HTL amount to 174% of the lipid entering it, which cannot be, because these esters can only come from lipid (HTL biocrude as a whole may exceed the lipid mass, since protein and carbohydrate also form biocrude, so the finding concerns the stated product chemistry, not the biocrude yield); and the aqueous phase carries 2.9 times the carbon of the feed, an elemental imbalance. Fourth, nitrogen and CO₂ supplies cover 0.6% and 0.45% of what 80 t/d of algae need, so the nutrient and carbon costs are missing.")
    d.fig("F02_productivity_check")
    d.p("The financial model has its own problems: three different total project costs (PHP 469, 534 and 1,048 million), loan and equity proceeds booked as year-0 inflows, inconsistent discount rates, crude glycerol sold at the price of refined glycerol, and a no-tax-holiday case whose cells return errors. Broken cell references (#REF!) also appear in the stream summary, energy balance and material balance sheets (check 24), so the workbooks cannot be recalculated as received and are not treated here as a functioning, verified financial model. Our draft 2026 working paper corrected the cash-flow treatment but kept the balance [@cereno2026].")
    d.h2("5.2 From the reported cost to the corrected design")
    br = BR
    d.p(f"«fig:F04_bridge» walks from the reported cost to a corrected design in six steps, holding the biomass that enters conversion at 24,000 t AFDW per year (80 t/d for 300 days, as in the original) so that only one thing changes at each step. The working paper's levelised cost of 0.67 EUR/L of FAME corresponds to {f2(br[0]['mfsp'])} USD per litre diesel-equivalent. Re-running the original basis on this engine gives {f2(br[1]['mfsp'])}, {pct(1 - br[1]['mfsp'] / br[0]['mfsp'])} below the reported value but of the same order, which shows that the low reported cost follows from the original assumptions rather than from the method. Replacing the original capital with a bottom-up estimate raises the cost to {f2(br[2]['mfsp'])}; growing the biomass at 20 g/m²/d, which needs {f0(br[3]['area'])} ha of ponds to deliver 24,000 t/yr to conversion after the 95% harvest recovery, raises it to {f2(br[3]['mfsp'])}. The HTL heat duty, hydrotreating and the purchase of nutrients bring it to {f2(br[6]['mfsp'])} USD/L deq. Two steps account for {pct((br[2]['mfsp']-br[1]['mfsp']+br[3]['mfsp']-br[2]['mfsp'])/(br[6]['mfsp']-br[1]['mfsp']))} of the increase: realistic capital and growing the algae.")
    d.fig("F04_bridge")
    d.table("bridge", "Bridge from the reported cost to the corrected design (HTL, 24,000 t AFDW/yr entering conversion).", ["Step", "MFSP, USD/L deq", "GWP, g CO₂e/MJ", "Pond area, ha", "TCI, USD M"],
            [[b["step"], f2(b["mfsp"]), ("–" if b["gwp"] != b["gwp"] else f1(b["gwp"])), f1(b["area"]), f1(b["tci"] / 1e6)] for b in br], widths=[3.6, 1, 1, 0.9, 0.9], align=["l", "r", "r", "r", "r"])
    d.h2("5.3 Verification of the engine")
    d.p(f"The engine passes {len(CLOS)} implementation and consistency checks on both base designs («tab:closure»). Several of them reproduce definitions (for example, upgraded fuel equals biocrude times the upgrading yield, or the fuel energy equals the product masses times their heating values); they confirm that the code computes what the equations state. Others bound a recycle against its source (recycled nitrogen against the nitrogen in the HTL aqueous phase), close the FAME split of harvested biomass into residue, returned extract and triglyceride, show that the NPV at the MFSP is zero to within 0.1% of capital, and that the impact contributions add up to the total. They do not demonstrate a complete, independently reconciled mass, elemental and energy balance: HTL solids and ash, carbon, nitrogen, upgrading water and off-gases are not closed element by element, and such a balance is a recommended next step. The implied biomass carbon content of 49.9% is within 3% of the elemental formula, and the HTL route puts {pct([c for c in CLOS if 'carbon efficiency' in c['check']][0]['engine'])} of the feed carbon into biocrude, within the 50–70% reported by PNNL [@jones2014,zhu2023]. All {len(CLOS)} checks pass.")
    rows = [[c["route"], c["block"], c["check"], (f"{c['engine']:,.4g}"), ("–" if c["reference"] != c["reference"] else f"{c['reference']:,.4g}"), ("–" if c["rel_dev"] != c["rel_dev"] else f"{100*c['rel_dev']:+.2f}%"), "Pass" if c["ok"] else "Fail"] for c in CLOS]
    d.table("closure", "Implementation and consistency checks of the engine (base designs).", ["Route", "Block", "Check", "Engine", "Reference", "Deviation", "Result"], rows, widths=[0.6, 0.7, 3.2, 0.9, 0.9, 0.7, 0.5], align=["l", "l", "l", "r", "r", "r", "l"], size=14)
    d.h2("5.4 Validation against published designs")
    d.p("The engine was run in the configuration of three published studies, with their prices, scale and assumptions, and compared with their results («fig:F25_validation», «tab:valid»). For the NREL 2023 farm state of technology the engine computes a minimum biomass selling price {pct(abs(VAL[0]['model']/654-1))} below the published unlined case on the alternative evaporation basis (654 USD/t) and {pct(abs(VAL[0]['model']/740-1))} below the base value (740 USD/t); for the PNNL whole-algae HTL design an MFSP {pct(abs(VAL[1]['model']/VAL[1]['published']-1))} below; and for the Argonne life-cycle assessment of the PNNL pathway a GWP {pct(VAL[2]['model']/VAL[2]['published']-1)} above [@klein2024,jones2014,pegallapati2015]. Deviations of this size are expected from differences in cost years, sub-models and inventory databases; the cost deviations lie inside the −30% to +50% accuracy range of an AACE Class 4 estimate [@aace2020].")
    rows = [[v["case"], f"{v['published']:,.3g}", f"{v['model']:,.3g}", f"{100*(v['model']/v['published']-1):+.0f}%", v["unit"], [{"citek": [v["source"]]}], v["note"]] for v in VAL]
    d.b.append({"k": "table", "tid": "valid", "bm": "tab_valid", "cap": ["Validation against published designs."], "head": [[h] for h in ["Case", "Published", "Engine", "Deviation", "Unit", "Source", "Configuration"]],
                "rows": [[(c if isinstance(c, list) else [str(c)]) for c in r] for r in rows], "widths": [1.5, 0.8, 0.7, 0.7, 1.2, 0.6, 2.8], "align": ["l", "r", "r", "r", "l", "l", "l"], "note": None, "size": 14})
    d.fig("F25_validation")

def tea(d):
    d.h1("6. Bottom-up techno-economic analysis", bm="sec_tea", pb=True)
    d.h2("6.1 Capital")
    d.p(f"Capital is estimated bottom-up from the equipment-level costs of the NREL farm and PNNL conversion designs, scaled with capacity exponents, escalated with the CEPCI to 2025 (813) and multiplied by a location factor of {B['loc_f']} for Southeast Asia [@klein2024,jones2014,davis2020,towler2022,cepci2026]:")
    d.eq("C = C_ref · (S / S_ref)^n · (CEPCI_2025 / CEPCI_ref) · f_loc", 4)
    d.p(f"with n = {B['exp_farm']} for the modular ponds and {B['exp_proc']} for process units. Installed costs are converted to fixed capital with the fixed-capital-to-installed ratios of the source designs: {B['tci_conv']} for conversion, from PNNL's fixed capital (467.9 million USD total capital less 22.3 million working capital) over 252.6 million installed, and {B['tci_farm']} for the farm, the NREL total-to-installed ratio of 1.64 with its 5% working capital removed (1.64/1.05). Working capital of {pct(B['wc'])} of fixed capital is then added once [@klein2024,jones2014]. Capital is in USD 2025 (CEPCI annual average); operating costs use 2026 prices without escalation between the two years. The result is an AACE Class 4 estimate, with an expected accuracy of −30% to +50% [@aace2020]. «fig:F07_capital» breaks down the capital of both base designs. The HTL plant needs {f0(H['tci']/1e6)} million USD, or {f0(H['tci']/H['area_ha']/1e3)} thousand USD per hectare of pond; {pct(sum(H['capf'].values())/(sum(H['capf'].values())+sum(H['capi'].values())))} of the installed cost is the farm.")
    d.fig("F07_capital")
    d.h2("6.2 Operating cost")
    d.p(f"Operating costs use 2026 Philippine prices where they exist («tab:prices»). Electricity is an estimated industrial tariff, {f2(B['p_el'])} USD/kWh, derived from the July 2026 residential rate of the Northern Negros cooperative and the 2021 ratio of its higher-voltage to residential rate; it is not a quoted tariff [@negrosnow2026,noneco2021]. Process heat from LPG costs {B['p_heat']:g} USD/GJ and from bagasse {B['p_heat_bio']:g} [@sunstar2026lpg,irena2012]. Fertilisers follow the World Bank September 2026 prices plus 15% for delivery [@wb2026]. Labour uses the Western Visayas wage order and PSA wage survey with 13 monthly payments and 35% benefits [@vds2025,psa2024ows]. Maintenance is {pct(B['maint'])} of installed capital per year and insurance with local taxes {pct(B['ins_tax'],1)} of capital [@jones2014]. «fig:F08_opex» shows the result.")
    keys = ["p_el", "p_heat", "p_heat_bio", "p_h2", "p_urea", "p_dap", "p_meoh", "p_hexane", "p_glyc_crude", "p_water", "p_land", "w_op", "p_cme", "p_diesel"]
    rows = [[REG.P[k]["name"], f"{REG.P[k]['base']:g}", f"{REG.P[k]['low']:g}–{REG.P[k]['high']:g}", REG.P[k]["unit"], REG.P[k]["conf"], [{"citek": REG.P[k]["src"]}]] for k in keys]
    d.b.append({"k": "table", "tid": "prices", "bm": "tab_prices", "cap": ["Prices used in the base case (2026 USD)."], "head": [[h] for h in ["Item", "Base", "Range", "Unit", "Conf.", "Sources"]],
                "rows": [[(c if isinstance(c, list) else [str(c)]) for c in r] for r in rows], "widths": [2.3, 0.8, 1.1, 1.1, 0.5, 1.6], "align": ["l", "r", "r", "l", "l", "l"], "note": None, "size": 15})
    d.fig("F08_opex")
    d.p(f"For HTL the largest operating costs are maintenance ({f1(H['cost']['Maintenance']/1e6)} M USD/yr), insurance and local taxes ({f1(H['cost']['Insurance & local tax']/1e6)}), electricity ({f1(H['cost']['Electricity']/1e6)}), nutrients ({f1(H['cost']['Nutrients']/1e6)}) and hydrogen ({f1(H['cost']['Hydrogen']/1e6)}); total operating cost is {f1(H['opex']/1e6)} M USD/yr. Capital-linked items make up over 40% of operating cost, so the plant's economics are dominated by capital.")
    d.h2("6.3 Discounted cash flow")
    d.p(f"The cash flow is real and unlevered, as in the NREL and PNNL designs: construction over three years (8%, 60% and 32% of fixed capital), a start-up year at 75% output, {B['life']} years of operation, straight-line depreciation over ten years, and corporate income tax of {pct(B['tax'])} under CREATE MORE after an assumed {B['ith']}-year income-tax holiday, which is conditional on registration of the project for incentives and is not an automatic entitlement [@davis2016,gt2025,pwc2026]. Tax losses are tracked by vintage: losses incurred during the holiday are not carried over, as the Tax Code bars deductions for losses incurred while exempt, and later losses are carried forward first-in first-out for {B['nolco_yr']} years under the enhanced NOLCO of registered enterprises, after which unused losses expire [@ra8424,firb2025]. The MFSP is the constant real fuel price at which the NPV at {pct(B['r'])} is zero, found by bisection:")
    d.eq("Σ_t [ (p·Q_t + R_t − C_t − T_t(p)) − I_t ] / (1 + r)^t = 0", 5)
    d.p("with Q_t fuel output, R_t co-product revenue, C_t operating cost, T_t tax (which depends on p), I_t investment and working capital. «fig:F09_cashflow» shows the cumulative discounted cash flow of both base designs at the CME price.")
    d.fig("F09_cashflow")
    d.h2("6.4 Results")
    d.p(f"The HTL base design has an MFSP of {f2(H['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD per litre diesel-equivalent ({f0(H['mfsp_gj'])} USD/GJ), {f1(H['mfsp_ldeq']/CME)} times the CME price and {f1(H['mfsp_ldeq']/DSL)} times the ex-tax diesel price. The FAME design reaches {f2(F['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L deq because it converts only {pct(F['fuel_mj']/(F['afdw']*22000))} of the biomass energy into fuel. Both plants have a negative NPV at the CME price ({f0(H['npv']/1e6)} and {f0(F['npv']/1e6)} M USD). The minimum biomass selling price, the cost of the algae at the farm gate, is {f0(H['mbsp'])} USD/t AFDW. «fig:F10_mfsp_benchmark» places the results among published designs: the HTL base sits above the PNNL and NREL targets because of its smaller scale (400 versus 2,000 ha), lower productivity (20 versus 25 g/m²/d) and Philippine utility prices.")
    d.fig("F10_mfsp_benchmark")

def lca(d):
    d.h1("7. Life cycle assessment", bm="sec_lca", pb=True)
    d.h2("7.1 Goal, scope and inventory")
    d.p("The LCA is attributional and follows ISO 14040/44 [@iso14040,iso14044]. Foreground flows from the engine are linked to ecoinvent 3.12 cut-off processes (database sheet 08_LCI) and characterised with IPCC 2021 GWP100 and the indicators of the EF 3.0 method (adapted), as implemented in the ecoinvent LCIA package used here [@ecoinvent312,wernet2016,forster2021,fazio2018]. The unit of each of the 183 indicators is listed in database sheet 09_LCIA. Impacts are computed directly from the unit-score matrix of the ecoinvent technosphere, h = U·f, where U holds the cradle-to-gate scores of 26,533 activities for 183 indicators and f is the foreground demand vector [@heijungs2002]. Electricity is the Visayas low-voltage mix of our companion Philippine grid model (1.137 kg CO₂e/kWh in 2024), a manuscript under review that cannot be checked independently from this package [@evidente2026grid]; the TEA prices a higher-voltage supply, so the low-voltage factor, which includes distribution losses, is a conservative proxy. Process heat uses a hybrid inventory: its GWP is set to an LPG/natural-gas furnace factor ({B['ef_heat_fossil']} kg CO₂e/MJ), while its other 182 indicators come from the ecoinvent natural-gas furnace dataset; bagasse heat uses the ecoinvent wood-chip furnace. Hydrogen is a proxy: the TEA prices an on-site LPG reformer (its price per kg includes reformer capital and operation), the LCA uses the ecoinvent market for low-pressure hydrogen with its GWP set to {B['ef_h2']} kg CO₂e/kg; pond N₂O follows the measurements of Guieysse et al. and Plouviez at 0.1% of nitrogen supplied [@guieysse2013,plouviez2017].")
    d.h2("7.2 Climate results")
    c = H["contrib"]; cf = F["contrib"]
    d.p(f"The HTL base design emits {f1(H['gwp'])} g CO₂e/MJ, a negligible deterministic saving ({H['saving']*100:.1f}%) against the RED II fossil comparator of 94 g/MJ [@red2]. Electricity contributes {f1(c['Electricity'])} g/MJ, nutrients {f1(c['Nutrients'])}, hydrogen {f1(c['Hydrogen'])}, process heat {f1(c['Heat'])}, pond N₂O {f1(c['Pond N2O'])} and infrastructure {f1(c['Infrastructure'])} («fig:F11_gwp_contrib»). The FAME design emits {f1(F['gwp'])} g/MJ, led by nutrients ({f1(cf['Nutrients'])}) and methane slip from digestion ({f1(cf['AD methane slip'])}); its electricity comes from biogas. Neither design reaches any of the RED II savings thresholds, which depend on when the installation started operating: 50% before October 2015, 60% from October 2015 to the end of 2020 and 65% from 2021 [@red2]. Meeting a numerical threshold would not by itself establish regulatory compliance, which also depends on the RED II calculation rules and certification.")
    d.fig("F11_gwp_contrib")
    d.p(f"The electricity result follows from how much power open ponds use: {f1(H['el_dem']/1e6)} GWh per year, or {H['el_dem']/H['fuel_mj']:.3f} kWh per MJ of fuel, against a grid that emits 1.14 kg CO₂e per kWh. Paddlewheel mixing alone uses {f1(H['el']['mixing']/1e6)} GWh. Earlier assessments likewise found the electricity mix decisive for the climate result of algal fuels [@quinn2015,pegallapati2015]. The fix is the electricity supply, not the process: Section 8 shows what a biomass or solar PPA does.")
    d.h2("7.3 Other impact categories")
    d.p("«fig:F12_ef_heatmap» compares fourteen EF 3.0 indicators across the main designs, as ratios to the HTL base. These vectors cover the background supply chains; direct foreground emissions (pond N₂O, methane slip, combustion) are counted only in the GWP of Section 7.2, which is why climate change is not repeated here, and for the 2050 design only electricity, hydrogen and fertiliser are prospective. Per MJ, the FAME route has 10% lower fossil resource use, but 2.6 times the supply-chain land use (3.7 times including the ponds, Table 2 of the manuscript) and water use and about 1.5 times the toxicity indicators, because it makes a third as much fuel from the same ponds. Switching the HTL plant to a biomass PPA cuts fossil resource use and acidification by 40% and freshwater eutrophication by 90%, but raises particulate matter 6.6-fold and the human toxicity indicators about 2-fold through bagasse combustion. The 2050 net-zero design is lower than the 2026 base in every category shown. The full set of 183 indicators per MJ for both base designs is in database sheet 09_LCIA.")
    d.fig("F12_ef_heatmap")

def scenarios(d):
    d.h1("8. Scenario analysis", bm="sec_scen", pb=True)
    d.p("Sixty scenarios, each recalculated in full through the balance, the TEA and the LCA, test six groups of questions («tab:scen»): A design variants (area, liners, aqueous-phase treatment, upgrading location, heat source, CO₂ source); B biology (productivity, yield, lipid, heat recovery, mixing energy); C energy (electricity supply and prices, clean utilities); D policy and finance (tax holiday, discount rate, plant life, prices, location factor, capital accuracy); E prospective years 2030–2050 under four energy pathways; and F accounting choices (allocation, CO₂ origin, the original basis).")
    rows = [[sid, SDEF[sid]["group"].split(". ")[1], SDEF[sid]["name"], f2(S[sid]["mfsp_ldeq"]), f1(S[sid]["gwp"]), f0(S[sid]["tci"] / 1e6), f2(S[sid]["ldeq"] / 1e6), f0(S[sid]["npv"] / 1e6)] for sid in sorted(S)]
    d.table("scen", "Results of the 60 scenarios (MFSP in USD/L deq; GWP in g CO₂e/MJ; TCI in USD M; output in ML deq/yr; NPV at the CME price in USD M).", ["ID", "Group", "Scenario", "MFSP", "GWP", "TCI", "Output", "NPV"], rows,
            widths=[0.45, 0.8, 3.4, 0.55, 0.55, 0.5, 0.6, 0.55], align=["l", "l", "l", "r", "r", "r", "r", "r"], size=13)
    d.fig("F13_scenarios"); d.fig("F14_scenario_map")
    d.p(f"Five results stand out («fig:F13_scenarios», «fig:F14_scenario_map»). First, scale matters, but not enough: going from 400 to 2,000 ha lowers the HTL MFSP from {f2(S['S01']['mfsp_ldeq'])} to {f2(S['S15']['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L deq, and the original 10.4 ha would cost {f2(S['S12']['mfsp_ldeq'])} for {f2(S['S12']['ldeq']/1e6)} ML/yr. Second, lining the ponds nearly doubles the cost ({f2(S['S03']['mfsp_ldeq'])}), so the unlined design of the NREL farm is essential [@klein2024]. Third, electricity supply decides the climate result: a hypothetical biomass PPA with San Carlos BioPower at the GEA-2 biomass tariff cuts the GWP to {f1(S['S27']['gwp'])} g/MJ, and a solar-plus-storage PPA to {f1(S['S28']['gwp'])} g/MJ (an LCA of PV generation only: battery manufacture, storage losses and backup supply are not included), while slightly lowering the cost, because the GEA tariff ({B['gea_bio']} PHP/kWh) is below the generation charge it replaces ({B['gen_vis']}) [@solarquarter2023,malaya2024rcoa]. Physical proximity and an auction tariff do not show that such a contract would be offered; these are scenarios pending evidence of supply. Adding biomass heat and low-carbon hydrogen reaches {f1(S['S32']['gwp'])} g/MJ, a {pct(S['S32']['saving'])} saving. Fourth, buying liquid CO₂ instead of using flue gas triples the GWP ({f1(S['S10']['gwp'])}), and treating the CO₂ as fossil would make the fuel worse than diesel ({f1(S['S58']['gwp'])}); the biogenic origin of the San Carlos CO₂ is a condition of the climate benefit. Fifth, the FAME route only approaches HTL when lipid is high and almost all of it is transesterifiable ({f2(S['S23']['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L deq at 27% lipid and 90% FAME yield).")
    d.p(f"Financial assumptions move the HTL MFSP between {f2(S['S34']['mfsp_ldeq'])} (6% discount rate) and {f2(S['S35']['mfsp_ldeq'])} (14%), and the capital accuracy band between {f2(S['S41']['mfsp_ldeq'])} and {f2(S['S42']['mfsp_ldeq'])}. The income-tax holiday is worth only {f2(S['S33']['mfsp_ldeq']-S['S01']['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L because, at the MFSP, depreciation shields most of the taxable profit in the holiday years. The accounting choice between energy allocation and system expansion does not change the HTL result, which has no non-fuel co-products, and lowers the FAME GWP from {f1(F['gwp'])} to {f1(S['S60']['gwp'])} g/MJ.")

def sensitivity(d):
    d.h1("9. Sensitivity analysis and tornado plots", bm="sec_sens", pb=True)
    d.p(f"One-at-a-time sensitivity moves each ranged parameter of the registry from its low to its high value while holding the others at base, for both routes ({2*len(OAT)} engine runs). The tornado plots in «fig:F15_tornado_htl_mfsp» to «fig:F18_tornado_fame_gwp» show the fifteen parameters with the largest swing; the value at each end of a bar is the parameter value that produces it.")
    d.fig("F15_tornado_htl_mfsp"); d.fig("F16_tornado_htl_gwp"); d.fig("F17_tornado_fame_mfsp"); d.fig("F18_tornado_fame_gwp")
    t = oat_top("HTL", "mfsp"); g = oat_top("HTL", "gwp"); f = oat_top("FAME", "mfsp")
    d.p(f"For the HTL MFSP, productivity has the widest swing ({f2(t[0]['mfsp_hi'])} to {f2(t[0]['mfsp_lo'])} USD/L deq over 30 to 10 g/m²/d), followed by HTL yield ({f2(t[1]['mfsp_hi'])}–{f2(t[1]['mfsp_lo'])}), discount rate, location factor, upgraded fuel yield and farm capital. For the HTL GWP the order changes: HTL yield ({f1(g[0]['gwp_hi'])}–{f1(g[0]['gwp_lo'])} g/MJ), mixing energy, CO₂ delivery energy, productivity, dewatering energy and the grid emission factor; apart from yield and productivity, which act through output, all act through electricity. For FAME the transesterifiable fraction of the lipid dominates ({f2(f[0]['mfsp_hi'])} to {f2(f[0]['mfsp_lo'])} USD/L deq), followed by productivity and lipid content. No single parameter brings either route to the CME price within its range. «fig:F19_spider» shows the response of the eight leading HTL parameters to changes of ±40%: productivity and yield act with diminishing returns, capital and discount rate almost linearly.")
    d.fig("F19_spider")

def breakeven(d):
    d.h1("10. Break-even and crossover analysis", bm="sec_be", pb=True)
    d.p(f"Break-even values were found by root-finding on the full engine («tab:be»). For the HTL base to sell at the CME price, productivity would need to reach {f0(be('Productivity for MFSP = CME price (HTL)'))} g/m²/d at 400 ha or {f0(be('Productivity for MFSP = CME price (HTL, 2,000 ha)'))} g/m²/d at about 2,000 ha (2,023 ha modelled), or the HTL yield would need to be {f2(be('Biocrude yield for MFSP = CME price (HTL)'))} kg/kg, above the theoretical maximum. Capital would need to fall to {pct(be('Capital multiplier for MFSP = CME (HTL)'))} of the estimate. No pond area achieves it at 20 g/m²/d. To match diesel before tax the productivity would have to be about {f0(be('Productivity for MFSP = fossil diesel ex-tax (HTL)'))} g/m²/d, which is physically impossible.")
    rows = [[r["item"], (f"{r['value']:,.3g}" if r["value"] == r["value"] else "not reached"), r["unit"], r["target"]] for r in CORE["breakeven"]]
    d.table("be", "Break-even and crossover values.", ["Quantity", "Value", "Unit", "Target"], rows, widths=[3.6, 0.9, 1.1, 1.3], align=["l", "r", "l", "l"])
    d.p(f"«fig:F20_breakeven_prod_area» maps the HTL MFSP over productivity and scale. The CME iso-line runs from about {f0(be('Productivity for MFSP = CME price (HTL)'))} g/m²/d at 400 ha to below 50 g/m²/d at 3,000–4,000 ha, everywhere at least twice the productivity measured outdoors. Plants above about 1,150 ha would also need CO₂ beyond what the 18 MW power plant emits (Section 16); purchased liquid CO₂ would add about 1 USD/L deq (S10). «fig:F21_breakeven_ef_yield» maps the GWP over the electricity emission factor and the HTL yield: the 50% saving needs a grid below {f2(be('Grid GWP for 50% GHG saving (HTL)'))} kg CO₂e/kWh at the base yield, which the Philippine grid reaches only in the net-zero pathway after 2045. The 65% saving cannot be reached through the grid alone because nutrients and hydrogen together contribute about 29 g/MJ. The crossover between routes is at a lipid content of {pct(be('Lipid content for FAME MFSP = HTL MFSP (crossover)'))}, above anything measured in wastewater-grown *Chlorella* [@esteves2022,safafar2016]; HTL is the cheaper route across the plausible range.")
    d.fig("F20_breakeven_prod_area"); d.fig("F21_breakeven_ef_yield")
    d.p(f"Expressed as a carbon price, closing the gap between the HTL MFSP and diesel would require about 2,000 USD per tonne of CO₂e or more: {f0(be('Carbon price closing the gap to diesel (HTL, solar PPA)'))} USD/t with a solar PPA; for the grid-powered base, whose saving is below 1%, an abatement cost is not defined. Microalgae fuel at this site is not a cost-effective abatement option in 2026.")

def prospective(d):
    d.h1("11. Prospective analysis to 2050", bm="sec_pros", pb=True)
    d.p("Prospective LCA and TEA require consistent assumptions about how the background and the technology change over time [@thonemann2020,sacchi2022,vandergiesen2020]. Four pathways were built («tab:pros»). Grid emission factors come from our Philippine grid model run on the Philippine Energy Plan 2023–2050 Reference and Clean Energy Scenarios 1 and 2, plus a net-zero sensitivity [@evidente2026grid,pep2023]. Productivity and HTL yield follow the trajectories of the US state-of-technology reports toward their design targets, faster in the more ambitious pathways [@klein2024,zhu2023]. Capital falls with a learning rate of 13% per doubling of cumulative capacity, the rate observed for maize ethanol [@hettinga2009], applied to an assumed number of doublings; both the rate, transferred from another biofuel, and the doublings are scenario assumptions. Hydrogen becomes cleaner and cheaper and fertiliser production less carbon-intensive.")
    from scen import PROS as PR, GRID_NAT, grid_ef
    rows = []
    for path in ("REF", "CES1", "CES2", "NZ"):
        rows.append([path, " / ".join(f"{grid_ef(path, y):.2f}" for y in (2030, 2040, 2050)), " / ".join(f"{v:g}" for v in PR["prod"][path][1:]), " / ".join(f"{v:g}" for v in PR["htl_y"][path][1:]),
                     " / ".join(f"{v:g}" for v in PR["doublings"][path][1:]), " / ".join(f"{v:g}" for v in PR["ef_h2"][path][1:]), " / ".join(f"{v:g}" for v in PR["p_h2"][path][1:])])
    d.table("pros", "Prospective pathways: values in 2030 / 2040 / 2050 (2026 values: grid 1.137, productivity 20, yield 0.41, H₂ 9.53 kg CO₂e/kg and 4.0 USD/kg).", ["Pathway", "Visayas grid, kg CO₂e/kWh", "Productivity, g/m²/d", "HTL yield", "Capacity doublings", "H₂ GWP, kg/kg", "H₂ price, USD/kg"], rows,
            widths=[0.8, 1.4, 1.2, 1.1, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0], align=["l", "r", "r", "r", "r", "r", "r"], size=14)
    d.fig("F22_prospective")
    d.p(f"«fig:F22_prospective» shows the annual results for HTL. Under the Reference pathway the MFSP does not cross the CME benchmark by 2050; it falls to {f2(S['S45']['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L deq by 2050 and the GWP to {f1(S['S45']['gwp'])} g/MJ, still above CME and short of the 50% saving. Under CES1 the MFSP falls below the fixed CME benchmark in {XOVER[('HTL','CES1')]} and reaches {f2(S['S48']['mfsp_ldeq'])} in 2050; under CES2 it crosses the benchmark in {XOVER[('HTL','CES2')]}, reaches {f2(S['S50']['mfsp_ldeq'])} in 2040 and {f2(S['S51']['mfsp_ldeq'])} in 2050, with a {pct(S['S51']['saving'])} GHG saving. Under the net-zero pathway it crosses in {XOVER[('HTL','NZ')]} and the MFSP is {f2(S['S53']['mfsp_ldeq'])} in 2040 and {f2(S['S54']['mfsp_ldeq'])} in 2050, and a plant of about 2,000 ha (2,023 ha modelled) with biomass heat reaches {f2(S['S56']['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L deq and {f1(S['S56']['gwp'])} g/MJ (S56), assuming free flue-gas CO₂ beyond what one 18 MW plant can supply. FAME stays above {f2(S['S55']['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L deq even in 2050. These results depend on productivity reaching 27–30 g/m²/d, which has not yet been demonstrated outdoors, and on the assumed number of capacity doublings; Section 12 shows how uncertain they are.")
